{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016M9wuiF1vXEhkwEob4sQTN","type":"tool_result","content":"the stripped code used. A component opts out with `{ client: true }` in\nsource or a `keep` list at build time. The parser is the `typescript`\npackage as an optional peer dependency. The transform ships as esbuild and\nVite plugins and as a `microbuild` CLI.\n\nImplementation is tracked under `.scratch/ssr-render-and-microbuild/`.\n\n## Consequences\n\nMarkup and behaviour have one source of truth, and a browser bundle\ncontains hydration code only. Other server templaters, such as Hono, can\ncall the static `render` directly.\n\nThe type declares `render` as always present while it is `undefined` at\nruntime after stripping; this gap is accepted and documented. The build\nstep is opt-in, so import-map consumers ship render code, consistent with\nADR-004. `client: true` is only recognised in an object literal, and the\nnon-chained form `const b = define('x'); b.render(fn)` is unsupported.\nThe transform adds `typescript` as a peer for anyone who uses it. The\ncore size budget stays at 3.5 kB, and the delta from the serializer is\nmeasured and reported.\n\n## Note (2026-10-04)\n\n[ADR-007](./ADR-007-client-templater.md) changes the templater's return\ntype: `html`, `h`, and `raw` return opaque markup instead of a string,\nand callers that need HTML convert with `String(m)`. The render function\nmay return markup. A component that the browser creates (from `mount` or\na reactive region) must be kept, with `client: true` or the build-time\nkeep list; creating a stripped component throws. This ADR is not\nsuperseded.\nstripped throws an error naming the tag and pointing at `client: true`\nand the build-time keep list. An adoption mismatch on tag name warns in\ndevelopment and leaves that server subtree untouched.\n\n## Trade-offs\n\nApp code gets one state object and no manual DOM work, at the cost of a\nlarger templater subpath. Consumers who do not import `microtags/htm`\npay nothing; the core entry grows only by the prop accessors.\n\nValue updates go straight through signals with no diffing. Only\nstructural changes (conditionals, lists) pay for a morph, and only over\nthe affected region.\n\nChanging the return type of `html` from string to markup is a breaking\nchange. It is acceptable at 0.0.x, and callers convert explicitly.\n\nComment markers add a few bytes to server output where reactivity is\nused, and nothing where it is not.\n\nTreating hosts as opaque keeps reconciliation simple and leaves a\ncomponent's internals to its own setup, but a region cannot patch inside\na host's markup.\n\n## Consequences\n\nADR-004 is relaxed for the templater subpath only. Its size-limit entry\nis raised to the measured result, and the browser build gets its own\nentry so growth stays visible. The core entry stays budgeted as before.\n\nNote (2026-10-14): both templaters brand `Markup` with\n`Symbol.for('microtags.markup')` and check the brand instead of\n`instanceof`, so markup from another copy of the package renders as\nmarkup, not escaped text. The browser build measured 4.61 kB after this\nchange, and its limit was raised to 4.65 kB. Merged with the adoption\nand morph changes it measured 4.71 kB, and the limit is 4.75 kB.\n\nADR-005 still holds: render links are stripped by default. A component\ncreated in the browser, for example as a list item, must be kept with\n`client: true` or the keep list.\n\nAfter a signal is assigned to a prop accessor, the host's attribute no\nlonger tracks the current value. Tests and docs must read the property,\nnot the attribute.\n\nA bound prop is read-only inside the component. Calling the setup prop\nsignal with a value while a signal or computed is bound throws an error\nnaming the tag and the prop, so a component cannot write page state\ndirectly and a write to a computed is not silently dropped. Unbound\nprops stay writable by their own component.\n\nThe package gains a fourth browser build script and a `browser`\ncondition on the `./htm` export. Bundler aliases that point\n`microtags/htm` at source must make the same split.\n\nThis ADR supersedes no earlier decision. ADR-008 records how the\nhydration example uses it.\n\n## Notes\n\n2026-10-14: component children originally shared the region marker.\nA kept component whose render had a reactive region before its\nchildren was then adopted against the wrong range, leaving the\nchildren's listeners unbound. Children now open with `<!--[c-->`.\nServer output changes only for components with non-empty children;\nstatic pages are byte-identical.\n\n2026-10-14: a multi-node `raw()` value is now adopted one DOM node per\nparsed node, with no skipping inside the span, so adoption stays\naligned after it. With passive adoption, the guarded `moveBefore` and\nthe duplicate key check, the browser templater measures 4.68 kB, and\nits size limit is 4.7 kB.\n### `builder.render(fn, options?)`\n\nAttach a render function that produces the component's inner HTML as a\nstring. The render function receives a context with `props` (plain\nresolved values, not signals), `refs`, `TAG`, `children` (inner HTML\nfrom the caller), and `attrs` (extra attributes from the caller).\n\n```ts\ndefine('my-counter')\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        start: p.number(),\n    }))\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        display: r.one<HTMLDivElement>(),\n        inc: r.one(),\n        dec: r.one(),\n    }))\n    .render(({ props, refs }) => {\n        return `\n            <div data-ref=\"${refs.display}\">\n                ${props.start}\n            </div>\n            <button data-ref=\"${refs.dec}\">-</button>\n            <button data-ref=\"${refs.inc}\">+</button>\n        `\n    })\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        // hydration code only\n    })\n```\n\nThe returned element class gains two statics: `render(props, options?)`\nwhich returns complete host markup, and `props`, the list of declared\nprop names. `options` accepts `children` (a string of inner HTML) and\n`attrs` (an object of extra attributes, all strings).\n\n`props` are the names declared in `.withProps`. They are serialized onto\nthe host tag with the prop's serializer, so `.setup` can coerce them back\non the client. `attrs` are any other attributes the caller put on the\ntag, such as `class`, `id`, or `aria-*`. They are written to the host tag\nas-is, and are available to the render function so it can forward them\ninside. See [Hydration](#hydration) for an example.\n\nPass `{ client: true }` to keep the render function in the client\nbundle after [`microbuild`](#microbuild) strips it:\n\n```ts\n.render(renderFn, { client: true })\n```\n\nSee [`client:true` vs `--keep`](#clienttrue-vs---keep) for when to\nuse each.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `.render` on the builder is unrelated to\n> [`microtags/render`](#microtagsrender), which is a DOM reconciler\n> for lists and single items.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n### `builder.setup(ctx)`\n## Subpath Exports\n\n### `microtags/context`\n\nCross-component context backed by alien-signals. A provider shares a value\nwith everything nested inside it; a consumer reads the value from its nearest\nprovider ancestor and reacts when it changes.\n\n */\nfunction toAttributes (attrs:Attrs):string\n```\n\n### `microtags/htm`\n\nA tagged-template string renderer that will expand any microtags\ninstances if finds. Exports `html`, `h`, `raw`, and the `Markup` type.\n`html`, `h`, and `raw` return `Markup`, not a string. Convert it with\n`String(m)` where a string is required, for example `innerHTML`. In the\nbrowser the same import resolves to a build that also exports `mount`;\nsee [ADR-007](./docs/adr/ADR-007-client-templater.md).\n\n```ts\nimport { html, raw } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { MyCounter } from './components.js'\n\nconst page = html`<div>\n    <${MyCounter} start=${5}>\n        <p>Extra children</p>\n    </${MyCounter}>\n    ${raw('<b>trusted markup</b>')}\n</div>`\n```\n\nWhen `html` meets a microtags element class, it splits props from\npassthrough attributes using the class's static `props` list, joins\nchildren into one string, and calls the static `render`. Plain string\ntag names emit ordinary HTML.\n\n**Escaping.** Every interpolated string is escaped (`<`, `>`, `&`, `\"`,\n`'`) before the parser sees it. Component output and `raw()` output\nare trusted and never re-escaped. Static template text is trusted.\n\n**Arrays.** Array children (from `.map()`) are flattened automatically.\n\n**Void elements.** Write void tags in the self-closing form, like\n`<br />`, `<img src=\"x\" />`, and `<input value=${v} />`. The output has\nno closing tag and no trailing slash, eg `<br>`. The `htm` parser does\nnot know which tags are void, so an unclosed `<br>` would be treated\nas an open tag and swallow the content after it. Instead of dropping\nthat content, the templater throws, on the server and in the browser,\nand the error names the tag and its self-closing form.\n\n**Custom elements.** A component tag always gets a closing tag in the\noutput, eg `<my-counter start=\"5\">...</my-counter>`, because HTML has no\nself-closing custom elements. In a template, close a component with\n`</${MyComponent}>` or with the `htm` shorthand `<//>`, which closes\nthe nearest open tag without repeating its name.\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/build`\n\nNode-only. Strips [`.render()`](#builderrenderfn-options) calls from\ndefine chains so the client bundle contains only hydration code.\n- [Serverside Example](#serverside-example)\n  * [`.TAG`](#tag)\n  * [`.refs`](#refs)\n- [Hydration](#hydration)\n  * [`.render`](#render)\n  * [`.html`](#html)\n  * [Stripping `.render` from the client bundle](#stripping-render-from-the-client-bundle)\n    + [1. Vite plugin](#1-vite-plugin)\n    + [2. esbuild plugin](#2-esbuild-plugin)\n    + [3. `microbuild` CLI](#3-microbuild-cli)\n    + [4. `transform` function](#4-transform-function)\n  * [Keeping `.render` on the client](#keeping-render-on-the-client)\n- [API](#api)\n  * [`define`](#define)\n    + [`ComponentBuilder`](#componentbuilder)\n  * [`builder.withProps`](#builderwithprops)\n    + [`p.json`](#pjson)\n    + [`p.schema`](#pschema)\n  * [`builder.withRefs(fn)`](#builderwithrefsfn)\n  * [`builder.withContexts`](#builderwithcontexts)\n    + [`.withContexts` Example](#withcontexts-example)\n  * [`builder.render(fn, options?)`](#builderrenderfn-options)\n  * [`builder.setup(ctx)`](#buildersetupctx)\n    + [`ctx`](#ctx)\n      - [`ctx` Methods](#ctx-methods)\n      - [`ctx.bind`](#ctxbind)\n- [Missing refs](#missing-refs)\n  * [Checking refs in tests](#checking-refs-in-tests)\n- [Typed Events](#typed-events)\n- [Templates](#templates)\n- [Subpath Exports](#subpath-exports)\n  * [`microtags/context`](#microtagscontext)\n  * [`microtags/render`](#microtagsrender)\n    + [`renderList`](#renderlist)\n      - [`renderList` Example](#renderlist-example)\n    + [`render`](#render)\n      - [`render` Example](#render-example)\n  * [`microtags/util`](#microtagsutil)\n    + [Coercion](#coercion)\n    + [`runAll`](#runall)\n    + [`toAttributes`](#toattributes)\n  * [`microtags/htm`](#microtagshtm)\n  * [`microtags/build`](#microtagsbuild)\n  * [`microtags/esbuild`](#microtagsesbuild)\n  * [`microtags/vite`](#microtagsvite)\n  * [`microbuild`](#microbuild)\n  * [`client:true` vs `--keep`](#clienttrue-vs---keep)\n- [Differences from `nanotags`](#differences-from-nanotags)\n  * [Reactivity primitive](#reactivity-primitive)\n  * [Effects + Subscriptions](#effects--subscriptions)\n  * [Refs: `r.all` instead of `r.many`](#refs-rall-instead-of-rmany)\n  * [Context API](#context-api)\n  * [Props](#props)\n  * [Not ported](#not-ported)\n  * [Additions / New Features](#additions--new-features)\n\n<!-- tocstop -->\n\n</details>\n\n## Install","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"10b72dcd-fe8f-4051-b896-6c085ba039d7","uuid":"9d402ae8-05fc-473d-8f39-34b31e2607dd","timestamp":"2026-10-15T04:28:36.736Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"the stripped code used. A component opts out with `{ client: true }` in\nsource or a `keep` list at build time. The parser is the `typescript`\npackage as an optional peer dependency. The transform ships as esbuild and\nVite plugins and as a `microbuild` CLI.\n\nImplementation is tracked under `.scratch/ssr-render-and-microbuild/`.\n\n## Consequences\n\nMarkup and behaviour have one source of truth, and a browser bundle\ncontains hydration code only. Other server templaters, such as Hono, can\ncall the static `render` directly.\n\nThe type declares `render` as always present while it is `undefined` at\nruntime after stripping; this gap is accepted and documented. The build\nstep is opt-in, so import-map consumers ship render code, consistent with\nADR-004. `client: true` is only recognised in an object literal, and the\nnon-chained form `const b = define('x'); b.render(fn)` is unsupported.\nThe transform adds `typescript` as a peer for anyone who uses it. The\ncore size budget stays at 3.5 kB, and the delta from the serializer is\nmeasured and reported.\n\n## Note (2026-10-04)\n\n[ADR-007](./ADR-007-client-templater.md) changes the templater's return\ntype: `html`, `h`, and `raw` return opaque markup instead of a string,\nand callers that need HTML convert with `String(m)`. The render function\nmay return markup. A component that the browser creates (from `mount` or\na reactive region) must be kept, with `client: true` or the build-time\nkeep list; creating a stripped component throws. This ADR is not\nsuperseded.\nstripped throws an error naming the tag and pointing at `client: true`\nand the build-time keep list. An adoption mismatch on tag name warns in\ndevelopment and leaves that server subtree untouched.\n\n## Trade-offs\n\nApp code gets one state object and no manual DOM work, at the cost of a\nlarger templater subpath. Consumers who do not import `microtags/htm`\npay nothing; the core entry grows only by the prop accessors.\n\nValue updates go straight through signals with no diffing. Only\nstructural changes (conditionals, lists) pay for a morph, and only over\nthe affected region.\n\nChanging the return type of `html` from string to markup is a breaking\nchange. It is acceptable at 0.0.x, and callers convert explicitly.\n\nComment markers add a few bytes to server output where reactivity is\nused, and nothing where it is not.\n\nTreating hosts as opaque keeps reconciliation simple and leaves a\ncomponent's internals to its own setup, but a region cannot patch inside\na host's markup.\n\n## Consequences\n\nADR-004 is relaxed for the templater subpath only. Its size-limit entry\nis raised to the measured result, and the browser build gets its own\nentry so growth stays visible. The core entry stays budgeted as before.\n\nNote (2026-10-14): both templaters brand `Markup` with\n`Symbol.for('microtags.markup')` and check the brand instead of\n`instanceof`, so markup from another copy of the package renders as\nmarkup, not escaped text. The browser build measured 4.61 kB after this\nchange, and its limit was raised to 4.65 kB. Merged with the adoption\nand morph changes it measured 4.71 kB, and the limit is 4.75 kB.\n\nADR-005 still holds: render links are stripped by default. A component\ncreated in the browser, for example as a list item, must be kept with\n`client: true` or the keep list.\n\nAfter a signal is assigned to a prop accessor, the host's attribute no\nlonger tracks the current value. Tests and docs must read the property,\nnot the attribute.\n\nA bound prop is read-only inside the component. Calling the setup prop\nsignal with a value while a signal or computed is bound throws an error\nnaming the tag and the prop, so a component cannot write page state\ndirectly and a write to a computed is not silently dropped. Unbound\nprops stay writable by their own component.\n\nThe package gains a fourth browser build script and a `browser`\ncondition on the `./htm` export. Bundler aliases that point\n`microtags/htm` at source must make the same split.\n\nThis ADR supersedes no earlier decision. ADR-008 records how the\nhydration example uses it.\n\n## Notes\n\n2026-10-14: component children originally shared the region marker.\nA kept component whose render had a reactive region before its\nchildren was then adopted against the wrong range, leaving the\nchildren's listeners unbound. Children now open with `<!--[c-->`.\nServer output changes only for components with non-empty children;\nstatic pages are byte-identical.\n\n2026-10-14: a multi-node `raw()` value is now adopted one DOM node per\nparsed node, with no skipping inside the span, so adoption stays\naligned after it. With passive adoption, the guarded `moveBefore` and\nthe duplicate key check, the browser templater measures 4.68 kB, and\nits size limit is 4.7 kB.\n### `builder.render(fn, options?)`\n\nAttach a render function that produces the component's inner HTML as a\nstring. The render function receives a context with `props` (plain\nresolved values, not signals), `refs`, `TAG`, `children` (inner HTML\nfrom the caller), and `attrs` (extra attributes from the caller).\n\n```ts\ndefine('my-counter')\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        start: p.number(),\n    }))\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        display: r.one<HTMLDivElement>(),\n        inc: r.one(),\n        dec: r.one(),\n    }))\n    .render(({ props, refs }) => {\n        return `\n            <div data-ref=\"${refs.display}\">\n                ${props.start}\n            </div>\n            <button data-ref=\"${refs.dec}\">-</button>\n            <button data-ref=\"${refs.inc}\">+</button>\n        `\n    })\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        // hydration code only\n    })\n```\n\nThe returned element class gains two statics: `render(props, options?)`\nwhich returns complete host markup, and `props`, the list of declared\nprop names. `options` accepts `children` (a string of inner HTML) and\n`attrs` (an object of extra attributes, all strings).\n\n`props` are the names declared in `.withProps`. They are serialized onto\nthe host tag with the prop's serializer, so `.setup` can coerce them back\non the client. `attrs` are any other attributes the caller put on the\ntag, such as `class`, `id`, or `aria-*`. They are written to the host tag\nas-is, and are available to the render function so it can forward them\ninside. See [Hydration](#hydration) for an example.\n\nPass `{ client: true }` to keep the render function in the client\nbundle after [`microbuild`](#microbuild) strips it:\n\n```ts\n.render(renderFn, { client: true })\n```\n\nSee [`client:true` vs `--keep`](#clienttrue-vs---keep) for when to\nuse each.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `.render` on the builder is unrelated to\n> [`microtags/render`](#microtagsrender), which is a DOM reconciler\n> for lists and single items.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n### `builder.setup(ctx)`\n## Subpath Exports\n\n### `microtags/context`\n\nCross-component context backed by alien-signals. A provider shares a value\nwith everything nested inside it; a consumer reads the value from its nearest\nprovider ancestor and reacts when it changes.\n\n */\nfunction toAttributes (attrs:Attrs):string\n```\n\n### `microtags/htm`\n\nA tagged-template string renderer that will expand any microtags\ninstances if finds. Exports `html`, `h`, `raw`, and the `Markup` type.\n`html`, `h`, and `raw` return `Markup`, not a string. Convert it with\n`String(m)` where a string is required, for example `innerHTML`. In the\nbrowser the same import resolves to a build that also exports `mount`;\nsee [ADR-007](./docs/adr/ADR-007-client-templater.md).\n\n```ts\nimport { html, raw } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { MyCounter } from './components.js'\n\nconst page = html`<div>\n    <${MyCounter} start=${5}>\n        <p>Extra children</p>\n    </${MyCounter}>\n    ${raw('<b>trusted markup</b>')}\n</div>`\n```\n\nWhen `html` meets a microtags element class, it splits props from\npassthrough attributes using the class's static `props` list, joins\nchildren into one string, and calls the static `render`. Plain string\ntag names emit ordinary HTML.\n\n**Escaping.** Every interpolated string is escaped (`<`, `>`, `&`, `\"`,\n`'`) before the parser sees it. Component output and `raw()` output\nare trusted and never re-escaped. Static template text is trusted.\n\n**Arrays.** Array children (from `.map()`) are flattened automatically.\n\n**Void elements.** Write void tags in the self-closing form, like\n`<br />`, `<img src=\"x\" />`, and `<input value=${v} />`. The output has\nno closing tag and no trailing slash, eg `<br>`. The `htm` parser does\nnot know which tags are void, so an unclosed `<br>` would be treated\nas an open tag and swallow the content after it. Instead of dropping\nthat content, the templater throws, on the server and in the browser,\nand the error names the tag and its self-closing form.\n\n**Custom elements.** A component tag always gets a closing tag in the\noutput, eg `<my-counter start=\"5\">...</my-counter>`, because HTML has no\nself-closing custom elements. In a template, close a component with\n`</${MyComponent}>` or with the `htm` shorthand `<//>`, which closes\nthe nearest open tag without repeating its name.\n\n---\n\n### `microtags/build`\n\nNode-only. Strips [`.render()`](#builderrenderfn-options) calls from\ndefine chains so the client bundle contains only hydration code.\n- [Serverside Example](#serverside-example)\n  * [`.TAG`](#tag)\n  * [`.refs`](#refs)\n- [Hydration](#hydration)\n  * [`.render`](#render)\n  * [`.html`](#html)\n  * [Stripping `.render` from the client bundle](#stripping-render-from-the-client-bundle)\n    + [1. Vite plugin](#1-vite-plugin)\n    + [2. esbuild plugin](#2-esbuild-plugin)\n    + [3. `microbuild` CLI](#3-microbuild-cli)\n    + [4. `transform` function](#4-transform-function)\n  * [Keeping `.render` on the client](#keeping-render-on-the-client)\n- [API](#api)\n  * [`define`](#define)\n    + [`ComponentBuilder`](#componentbuilder)\n  * [`builder.withProps`](#builderwithprops)\n    + [`p.json`](#pjson)\n    + [`p.schema`](#pschema)\n  * [`builder.withRefs(fn)`](#builderwithrefsfn)\n  * [`builder.withContexts`](#builderwithcontexts)\n    + [`.withContexts` Example](#withcontexts-example)\n  * [`builder.render(fn, options?)`](#builderrenderfn-options)\n  * [`builder.setup(ctx)`](#buildersetupctx)\n    + [`ctx`](#ctx)\n      - [`ctx` Methods](#ctx-methods)\n      - [`ctx.bind`](#ctxbind)\n- [Missing refs](#missing-refs)\n  * [Checking refs in tests](#checking-refs-in-tests)\n- [Typed Events](#typed-events)\n- [Templates](#templates)\n- [Subpath Exports](#subpath-exports)\n  * [`microtags/context`](#microtagscontext)\n  * [`microtags/render`](#microtagsrender)\n    + [`renderList`](#renderlist)\n      - [`renderList` Example](#renderlist-example)\n    + [`render`](#render)\n      - [`render` Example](#render-example)\n  * [`microtags/util`](#microtagsutil)\n    + [Coercion](#coercion)\n    + [`runAll`](#runall)\n    + [`toAttributes`](#toattributes)\n  * [`microtags/htm`](#microtagshtm)\n  * [`microtags/build`](#microtagsbuild)\n  * [`microtags/esbuild`](#microtagsesbuild)\n  * [`microtags/vite`](#microtagsvite)\n  * [`microbuild`](#microbuild)\n  * [`client:true` vs `--keep`](#clienttrue-vs---keep)\n- [Differences from `nanotags`](#differences-from-nanotags)\n  * [Reactivity primitive](#reactivity-primitive)\n  * [Effects + Subscriptions](#effects--subscriptions)\n  * [Refs: `r.all` instead of `r.many`](#refs-rall-instead-of-rmany)\n  * [Context API](#context-api)\n  * [Props](#props)\n  * [Not ported](#not-ported)\n  * [Additions / New Features](#additions--new-features)\n\n<!-- tocstop -->\n\n</details>\n\n## Install","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016M9wuiF1vXEhkwEob4sQTN","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"c